.SH "NAME"
imcat \- image concatenator for text terminals.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.HP \w'\fBimcat\fR\ 'u
\fBimcat\fR [\fB\-w\fR\ \fICOLUMNS\fR] [\fB\-b\fR\ \fI#RRGGBB\fR] \fIIMAGE\fR...
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBimcat\fR
displays the specified images on a text terminal that supports 24-bit colour.
Images wider than the terminal are scaled down by averaging every pixel;
smaller images are shown at their native size.
Photos are rotated according to their EXIF orientation.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-width\fR \fICOLUMNS\fR
.RS 4
Maximum output width in columns. Defaults to the width of the terminal
attached to standard output, standard error or standard input, in that order,
or 80 columns when none is a terminal.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-bg\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR
.RS 4
Background colour of the terminal. Transparent areas of images are blended
into this colour. Defaults to black.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
.RS 4
Shows command line syntax.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
.RS 4
Shows the version.
.RE
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.PP
\fBIMCAT_WIDTH\fR
.RS 4
Default for \fB\-\-width\fR.
.RE
.PP
\fBIMCATBG\fR
.RS 4
Default for \fB\-\-bg\fR. For instance, when using a white terminal background:
.PP
$ export IMCATBG="#ffffff"
.br
$ imcat kitten.png
.RE
.PP
Options given on the command line override these variables.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
0 if every image was displayed, 1 if any image could not be loaded,
2 for invalid options.
